    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d404">

                <pb facs="0586" n="510" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg510"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.151/120a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacVeagh</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-07-06T15:00:00-04:00">July 6, 1934—3 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>42. Ulen and Company has informed the Department that Greek Government is
                    threatening to seize Company’s assets in Greece if <pb facs="0587" n="511"
                        xml:id="pg511"/> Company does not refund by July 13th approximately $100,000
                    claimed by Government as payment to Company in excess of amounts due it under
                    waterworks construction contract. Company states that sum in question was
                    received in partial payment of extra fees for supplemental work undertaken and
                    completed in agreement with Government and that instead of having been overpayed
                    approximately $100,000 Government still owes it approximately $45,000 in the
                    form of extra fees for supplemental work.</p>
                <p>Unless another course of action recommends itself to you please request personal
                    interview with the Greek Minister for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d404-f1"/> and inform him that Company has replied to demand for
                    restitution of sum in question by letter mailed from New York June 29th; that
                        Gausmann<anchor n="29" xml:id="d404-f2"/> will arrive in Athens about July
                    30 empowered to seek settlement; and that it is the hope of both this Government
                    and the Company that an amicable settlement can be arrived at within the
                    framework of the contract. You may in your discretion add that this Government
                    would, in the light of its present understanding of the case, take a serious
                    view of arbitrary action at this time by the Greek Government prejudicial to the
                    Company’s interest.</p>
                <p>Please submit telegraphic report.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
